{"kind":"assert","text":"The first implementation of a ratified decision is unmergeable, and the cause is not in the change.\n\nMEASURED. Head 4a72921c9c2f9626cd627bc23adfef8f32fcd113 implements ratified decision a85509db. Its artifacts 8983f3e3 at internal/workroom and c9bee417 at docs were both ruled effective and both project stale, immediately, with nothing between filing and reading them. They are stale because they rest on a85509db, which is itself stale: its bases include 8ad2d6f5, which is retired, and the declared-kinds chain beneath it carries several more retired artifacts. None of that history concerns the promise rule.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. validateMerge in cmd/gs/main.go calls liveArtifact, which returns \"artifact is stale\" and refuses. So an approval on this head, however careful, cannot be acted on by gs merge. gs review is the softer gate and will proceed while recording the staleness, so the review can happen; the merge cannot.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT AVOIDABLE BY BETTER FILING. The repository discipline says self-initiated work rests directly on the motivating ratified decision, and a85509db is that decision — it is the only thing that authorises this change to exist. Choosing a different basis to dodge the staleness would be choosing a basis that did not motivate the work, which is a worse record. I checked whether a live alternative existed before concluding this; there is none, because what makes the work legitimate is exactly the thing that is stale.\n\nWHAT THIS IS EVIDENCE FOR, AND WHAT I AM NOT DOING WITH IT. This is the case proposal c719ab1e describes: world-staleness propagating along request, promise and report edges that describe no code, then the merge gate refusing on staleness in general rather than on the narrower fact. Hugh deferred that decision at 2026-08-11 21:49 in assert 57f329de, behind the conversation-workflow model, and told us not to implement, ratify, or treat it as policy until that settles. I am not reopening it and I am not asking for it to be reconsidered. I am recording that the deferral has a measured price which was not visible when it was made: it is not only a drag on old records, it blocks new work authorised by a decision hugh himself ratified six hours earlier.\n\nWHAT I SUGGEST INSTEAD, FOR WHOEVER PICKS THIS UP. Do not special-case the gate. Either the conversation-workflow model settles and c719ab1e is reconsidered against it, as hugh directed, or someone with ratifier authority decides separately how a head in this position reaches main. Both are decisions, not repairs, and neither is mine to take.\n\nRecorded so this survives the session and so the next actor does not spend the diagnosis again."}